  "summary": "Portuguese colonial architecture in Brazil with significant local adaptations and influences.",
  "description": "Features whitewashed walls, blue tiles, azulejos, and baroque elements blended with indigenous techniques and materials.",
  "culturalContext": "Portuguese colonial building in Brazil — from 16th-century Olinda through the 18th-century Minas Gerais gold towns — adapted Lisbon's baroque and convent plainness to tropical scale, with thick whitewashed walls, azulejo tile facades, and wood ceilings against humidity. Aleijadinho's Ouro Preto churches crown the tradition; UNESCO-listed Ouro Preto, Olinda and Paraty preserve whole urban fabrics of the style.",
